<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abraham Crijnssen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1669">1669</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abraham Crijnssen was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> naval commander from Zeeland. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a> he led a Zeeland squadron to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and in February 1667 captured the English colony of Willoughbyland on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname-river">Suriname River</a>, renaming its fort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-zeelandia-taiwan">Fort Zeelandia</a>. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> that year left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> in Dutch hands, beginning more than three centuries of Dutch rule. He died in 1669.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-crijnssen.jpg" alt="Abraham Crijnssen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Don S. Montgomery, USN(Ret) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ABRAHAM_CRIJNSSEN_(F-816).JPEG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Crijnssen, also written Krijnssen, was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a>; his date of birth is unknown. In 1632 he commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samson">Samson</a> and the Vlissingen, two ships of a fleet of twelve privateers owned by the Lampsins brothers, did much damage to the Dunkirk Privateers and distinguished himself at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-downs">Battle of the Downs</a> in 1639. By 1665 he was a commander at the Admiralty of Zeeland, serving first as first captain under Adriaan Banckert, and he fought at the Four Days' Battle and the St. James's Day Battle in command of the frigate Zeelandia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In December 1666 he was given a squadron of the frigates Zeelandia, West-Cappelle and Zeeridder and four smaller ships for an expedition against the English in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veere">Veere</a> on 30 December with 700 men, more than 200 of them soldiers. He reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname-river">Suriname River</a> and Fort Willoughby on 25 February 1667. The planters were allowed to stay with equal citizenship if they swore allegiance to the States of Zeeland, and he left a garrison in the fort.</p><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, where he rebuilt the destroyed fort, and on 4 May to Sint Eustatius, which he retook. Off Nevis, joined by a French fleet, he had to break off a battle with a strong English force because the allies worked poorly together. In the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-river">James River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> he then surprised an English tobacco fleet, captured its escort warship, manned 11 merchant ships with his crews and burned the rest. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a> on 25 August, he was welcomed as a hero and given a golden chain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The English had retaken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-zeelandia-taiwan">Fort Zeelandia</a> in October 1667, despite the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, so in February 1668 Crijnssen was sent back with three ships. He arrived on 20 April, and by 28 April all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> was again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> hands. He stayed on as acting governor until his death on 1 February 1669 and was succeeded by Philip Lichtenberg. The colony remained Dutch until 1975, and a minesweeper and a frigate of the Dutch navy have been named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abraham Crijnssen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second Anglo-Dutch War a fleet from the Dutch province of Zeeland under <b>Abraham Crijnssen</b> captured its main fort in February 1667 and renamed it Fort Zeelandia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1667, during the Second Anglo-Dutch War, a Zeeland fleet under <b>Abraham Crijnssen</b> captured the colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/willoughbyland">Willoughbyland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.45,3.58&amp;year=1659" aria-label="Vlissingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4631 382 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abraham Crijnssen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5081 652h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5081 652h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.45,3.58&amp;year=1659">What was here in 1659? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
